# SYNOPSIS

```ndb
match=pci class=0x010802 program=/boot/bin/drv-nvme post=disk# msi=8
```

```text
/srv/disk0, /srv/disk1, ...
drv-nvme.reset=n  drv-nvme.die=n  drv-nvme.fault=1
```

# DESCRIPTION

`drv-nvme` drives an NVM Express controller, as the base specification 1.4 describes it, and serves the controller's first namespace as a disk of the block class on its post in `/srv`.

devmgr(8) starts it for every PCI function of class 01:08:02, an NVMe I/O controller, whoever made it, by the manifest in `/boot/drv/nvme.ndb` shown above.
The driver gets the function's configuration space, its memory BARs, a DMA domain, up to eight MSI-X interrupts (`msi=8`), and the listen end of its post, and nothing else; it takes no arguments.
The post is numbered with the other disks in the order devmgr finds the functions on the bus, whichever driver serves them: `/srv/disk0` is the first disk found, virtio or NVMe.
partd(8) holds a connector to it, and hands out its partitions as posts of their own, which fsd(4) and the other file servers use.

At start it reads the controller's limits and brings it up, then identifies the controller, the namespaces it has, and the first namespace's size and format.
It refuses, and exits, if there is no register BAR of at least 8 KiB, no MSI-X, no NVM command set, no support for 4 KiB memory pages, no namespace, a namespace format with metadata, or a sector size outside 512 to 4096 bytes.
It then asks for eight I/O queue pairs and makes as many as the controller grants.
The admin queue, 32 entries, uses vector 0 and is polled; the I/O queues share the other vectors in turn, or vector 0 when there is only one.
A queue must be physically contiguous, so each is as deep as the controller allows (`CAP.MQES`), 256 at most, and as one contiguous run of the driver's memory gives the device: 64 entries through a pass-through DMA domain, 256 through VT-d or SMMUv3.

## Sessions

The block class is defined in `docs/proto/block.md`; in short:
a client sends `CONNECT` on the post, naming a window of the disk (a first sector and a count, 0 for the rest) and whether it is read-only, and gets back a ring session (see ring(2)) that reaches that window and nothing else.
Each request on the ring is one submission, completed by one completion with the same `user_data`, in any order.
The driver serves 8 sessions at once, each given to an I/O queue pair in turn, so sessions share pairs when there are fewer than 8.
A `CONNECT` it cannot take is refused: `INVALID` for a message that is not one, `RANGE` for a window outside the disk, `NO_MEMORY` when 8 sessions are open.

The client arena, 1 MiB, is given to the controller through the DMA domain when the session opens, and every transfer names the arena's own pages, so nothing is copied.
The requests:

: `INFO`
  Completes at once with the most bytes one transfer moves (128 KiB, or less if the controller's `MDTS` says so), the sector size, the window's sectors, and the flags `INFO_READONLY` for a read-only session, `INFO_CACHE` if the controller has a volatile write cache, and `INFO_DISCARD` if it takes Dataset Management.
: `READ`, `WRITE`
  An NVM Read or Write of whole sectors, its PRPs made from the arena's pages: the second page, or a list of the rest.
  A transfer that is empty, not a multiple of the sector size, not aligned to one in the arena, or longer than `INFO` says completes with `INVALID`; one outside the window or the arena with `RANGE`; a write in a read-only session with `ACCESS`; none of these reaches the controller.
: `WRITE_FUA`
  A Write with Force Unit Access set.
: `FLUSH`
  An NVM Flush; it completes at once in a read-only session or when the controller has no volatile write cache.
: `DISCARD`
  A Dataset Management deallocate of one range of at most 2^32−1 sectors; what lies beyond is left as it is, since a discard is a hint.
  It completes at once, as done, if the controller cannot discard.

A controller error completes the request with `IO`.
While a session's queue pair is full the driver stops taking its submissions, and takes them again as commands complete.
A session that does not drain its completions is dropped.
When a session goes with commands in flight, its arena stays given to the controller until they complete.

## Recovery

A command not done 10 seconds after it was submitted is aborted with the admin command Abort.
If it is still not done 5 seconds later, or the controller reports a fatal status (`CSTS.CFS`), the driver resets the controller: it disables and enables it, makes its queues again, and submits again every command that was in flight, under its old id.
Reads, writes of the same bytes, flushes and discards may be done twice without harm, so the clients see a delay rather than an error.
If the controller does not come back after four tries, the driver exits.

If the driver exits, devmgr revokes its DMA domain, turns off bus mastering, resets the function where it can, and starts it again on the same post, with nothing kept; clients see their sessions close, dial again and submit again what had not completed (see `docs/proto/block.md` §4).
devmgr gives up after five starts.

## Options

The driver reads its options from the kernel command line, words of the form `drv-nvme.`<key>`=`<value>, which devmgr passes it.
They are for tests:

: `drv-nvme.reset=`<n>
  Reset the controller after every <n> completions, with commands in flight.
: `drv-nvme.die=`<n>
  On its first start only, exit after <n> completions, as a crash would, with commands in flight.
: `drv-nvme.fault=1`
  Before serving, aim an Identify at a device address the DMA domain does not map, and report whether the IOMMU stopped it and counted the fault.

# EXAMPLES

What devmgr and the driver print at boot, on x86_64 under QEMU with an NVMe disk of 64 MiB (here and below, a line too long for the page is broken and indented):

```text
devmgr: 00:03.0 1b36:0010 storage msi-x bar0=16K
devmgr: started drv-nvme
drv-nvme: 64 MiB, 512-byte sectors, 8 queues of 256, 8 vectors,
    write cache, discard
```

With `drv-nvme.die=100` on the command line, the restart, and the block client in fsd dialling again:

```text
drv-nvme: exiting, as asked (drv-nvme.die)
devmgr: /boot/bin/drv-nvme exited: drv-nvme.die
devmgr: started drv-nvme
drv-nvme: 64 MiB, 512-byte sectors, 8 queues of 256, 8 vectors,
    write cache, discard
vx-blk: the disk's session went; dialled again, and the request done
    again
```

With `drv-nvme.fault=1`:

```text
drv-nvme: a DMA fault, as asked: the command completed, the fault
    reported, 1 counted
```

